schema: claim-evidence
object:
  title: "Algorithmic/unpaid contributor-reward models produced burnout and quiet capture in SourceCred and Gitcoin"
  type: claim-evidence
  claim: >-
    SourceCred's algorithmic contribution-scoring "CredSperiment" led members
    to optimize for cred instead of mission, concentrated soft power in
    whoever steered the scoring weights, and wound down by 2022. Gitcoin's
    volunteer steward model (governance power, no pay, no clear duties) burned
    out the few active stewards while most went passive; Gitcoin's 2025
    governance revamp replaced unpaid stewards with compensated delegates with
    explicit, renewable responsibilities.
  evidence: >-
    Cited to Ellie Rennie's ethnography of the SourceCred CredSperiment and
    Gitcoin's own 2025 Governance Manual describing the revamp.
  source_lineage: src/content/docs/conflict-resolution.md
  interpretation: >-
    Used as the article's cautionary example for "the slow-burn conflict":
    bolting a rewards algorithm or unpaid-but-powerful role onto a group that
    has not first agreed what it values corrodes trust gradually rather than
    exploding.
  uncertainty: >-
    Drawn from two secondary sources (an academic-style ethnography and the
    organization's own retrospective framing of its revamp), not independently
    cross-verified against other participant accounts.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/conflict-resolution.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  work_order: wo-ee6204d14f2b
